WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:01.110
Adam Cushman, Actor/Filmmaker/Author: Okay,
cry now.

00:00:01.110 --> 00:00:04.830
Now you’re going to cry.

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:05.830
Ready…cry.

00:00:05.830 --> 00:00:11.309
Film Courage: What are five things a director
should never do on set?

00:00:11.309 --> 00:00:12.309
Adam: Yell.

00:00:12.309 --> 00:00:14.539
Film Courage: Unless it’s “Action!”

00:00:14.539 --> 00:00:18.990
Adam: Yeah but even that, do you need to yell
it?

00:00:18.990 --> 00:00:24.160
Are you going to rattle the actors right before
they’re supposed to act?

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:30.860
I don’t know, I’m all about the quiet
action.

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:37.880
Line readings, don’t do that.

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:39.990
Just be specific with the actors.

00:00:39.990 --> 00:00:41.051
Be like “Okay, cry now.

00:00:41.051 --> 00:00:44.940
Now you’re going to cry.

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:45.940
Read…cry.”

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:48.550
Give them something.

00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:50.940
Explain the situation.

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:55.260
Actors want you to explain the situation to
them rather then give them the result.

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:58.780
I think that’s key.

00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:00.820
Don’t over shot list.

00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:03.380
That’s another thing.

00:01:03.380 --> 00:01:08.390
In the beginning you tend to do that and you
really want to know like the main thing is

00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:11.470
I’m going to show up and I want to know
what I am doing and I want everyone to see

00:01:11.470 --> 00:01:15.800
that I’ve got my stuff together and you
tend to overcompensate with the shot list

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:18.730
but you’ve got to let go of that.

00:01:18.730 --> 00:01:26.430
I don’t even do it anymore because I found
the best way for the way I work is to come

00:01:26.430 --> 00:01:31.950
in, let the actors do their rehearsal and
then block it and block according to what

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:36.760
they do and you will still find that it’s
similar to what you envisioned.

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:40.479
You are not like “Okay we are going to do
a close-up, medium close-up, over the shoulder

00:01:40.479 --> 00:01:45.490
, around the side, master shot, four takes
of that for which you will never use.”

00:01:45.490 --> 00:01:47.290
You know what I mean?

00:01:47.290 --> 00:01:53.070
And doing that it makes your day go faster
as well and it’s just better for everyone.

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:54.200
So that’s three?

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:56.070
Film Courage: Do you storyboard?

00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:59.700
Adam: I don’t know how to draw.

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:03.810
I wish I knew how to draw.

00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:05.310
I would.

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:09.810
But no, I’ve never done that.

00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:12.069
Film Courage: So…three…okay.

00:02:12.069 --> 00:02:18.061
But it sounds like you feel out a lot of situations
intuitively and so you don’t always necessarily

00:02:18.061 --> 00:02:19.650
plan things?

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:22.739
Because things are going to change all the
time on a film set.

00:02:22.739 --> 00:02:25.200
All sorts of stuff is going to happen.

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:26.200
People are going to not show up.

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:28.270
Maybe they show up and they’re having a
bad day.

00:02:28.270 --> 00:02:30.360
All different things are going to happen.

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:31.360
Adam: Right.

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:32.360
Right.

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:38.010
I mean plan but leave room to work so you
are in the flow.

00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:38.510
Question For The Viewers: What are three things
a director should never do on set?

